IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT domain
IMPORT `g7-interpretation`
IMPORT `g7-vintages`
IMPORT `g7-commencement`
IMPORT `g7-art37-43-final`
IMPORT `g7-interpretation-laws`

-- g7-tests-interpretation-laws: tests that two forks of group G7 are settled by the text of two other Laws, the Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954 and the Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021.
--   F7.11  how the day named as the day of commencement is counted.  Reading taken: the named day is the first day in force.  The other reading: the day after the named day is.
--   F7.14  whether an Order under Article 2(2) can make consequential amendments of other provisions of the Law.  Reading taken: only Regulations can.  The other reading: an Order can too.
-- The two Laws are reached through the hand-made copies of g7-interpretation-laws (1954 Article 1(5) and 1(6); 2021 Articles 2, 3(2), 3(6), 9(5) and 9(6)).
-- Every expected value below was written by hand, before the module was run, from the text of the Law cited in the comment above it, or from the Table of Legislation History of the Charities Law for a date.
-- No expected value was copied from what the code returned.  Where a Charities assertion is compared with a Law's answer, both sides are asserted against the hand-written value as well.
--
-- Which Law governs a day.
-- The 1954 Law, Article 1(5), in the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021: "Where an enactment is expressed or construed as being expressed to come into operation on a particular day, it shall be construed as coming into operation
-- immediately on the expiration of the previous day."  So an enactment that names a day comes into operation at the start of that day, and that day is its first day in force.
-- The 2021 Law, Article 3(2): "If any Jersey legislation is expressed to come into force on a particular day, it is treated as coming into force at the beginning of that day, unless provision is made to the contrary."
-- The 2021 Law, Article 3(6): "Nothing in this Article affects the commencement of any Jersey legislation that had commenced before the commencement of this Part."  The Law, Part 2 included, came into force on 28 September 2021 (endnotes, Table of Legislation History).
-- So an enactment that commenced before 28 September 2021 is outside Article 3 and is governed by the 1954 Law (Articles 1(5) and 1(6) were deleted from 28 September 2021, by the 2021 Law),
-- and one that commenced on or after that day is governed by Article 3 of the 2021 Law.
--
-- What the 1954 text is for the days before 1 January 2019.
-- The text of the 1954 Law for the days before 1 January 2019 is not deposited.  Articles 1(5) and 1(6) are answered for those days (from 28 March 2003) by inference from the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation
-- (L.16/2003 amended Article 1(5) and inserted Article 1(6); in force 28 March 2003), which the 1954 subject labels "inferred from the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation, not verified against L.16/2003".
-- The enactments that brought the three stages of the Charities Law into force were all made before 1 January 2019 (the Law as enacted for stage 1, R&O.17/2018, made on 6 March 2018, for stages 2 and 3), so the text of the 1954 Law
-- in force when they were made is the inferred one, and the stages are tested with that basis.  The basis only labels an answer: the day an answer carries is the same on either basis.
--
-- What is not tested here, and why.
-- R&O.10/2024 comes into force at 9.30 a.m. on 27 February 2024.  It states a time of day, which is fork F7.1 and stays open: it is not tested here.
-- The three Orders that come into force "7 days after it is made" (R&O.28/2023 Article 3, R&O.56/2025 Article 2, R&O.102/2026 Article 7(6)) name no day in their own words; how the seven days are counted is fork F7.2, which these Laws do not settle.
-- They are tested in the group "Table rows that state a period" below only for what holds whichever way the seven days are counted.
-- Whether the 2021 Law's Article 3(2) applies to an enactment that says "7 days after it is made" is assumed there, not ruled.

§ `Interpretation Laws -- tests`

`fav` MEANS `the favoured interpretation`
`other F7.11` MEANS `the interpretation with the other reading of F7.11`
`other F7.14` MEANS `the interpretation with the other reading of F7.14`

-- The readings of the 2021 Law that the tests of groups 1 and 2 pass and that those tests never read: L2 is read only when an enactment states a time of day, and L3 only for a day that is 28 September 2021.
`the first reading of L2` MEANS `a stated time of day is provision to the contrary, and the legislation comes into force at that time`
`the first reading of L3` MEANS `the beginning of 28 September 2021, by Article 3(2)`

-- The two bases on which the 1954 Law's Article 1(5) and 1(6) are read.
`the inferred text` MEANS `inferred from the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation, not verified against L.16/2003`
`the deposited text` MEANS `read from the deposited text in force on that day`

-- An enactment expressed to come into operation on a particular day, in the nouns of the 1954 Law.
-- The day the enactment was passed or made is a required input, but Article 1(5) reads only the day the enactment names; the tests say where the sources do not show the day made and a stand-in is passed.
GIVEN named IS A DATE
      made IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Commencement as stated`
`a commencement naming the day` named made MEANS
    `Commencement as stated` WITH
        `how it is expressed` IS `on a particular day`
        `the day it names` IS JUST named
        `the day it was passed or made` IS made
        `the day the question is asked` IS NOTHING

-- The day that the copy of 1954 Article 1(5) and 1(6) gives, without the basis it carries.
GIVEN c IS A `Commencement as stated`
      basis IS A `Basis of a reading`
GIVETH A DATE
`the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` c basis MEANS
    LET r MEANS `Article 1(5) and 1(6): the day under the text` c basis IN
        r's `the day`

-- The first day in force that the Charities row gives to an amending instrument of its own Table, under a reading of F7.11 (and of F7.2, which only the rows that state a period read).
GIVEN instrument IS AN `Amending instrument`
      interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 21 to 25, 37 to 43 and the endnotes`
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` instrument interp MEANS
    `the first day on which a commencement is in force` (instrument's `comes into force`) (instrument's `made on`) interp

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- (1) F7.11: the three stages of the Charities Law
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§ `Group 1: the three stages (F7.11)`

-- Table of Legislation History, row L.41/2014: "Parts 1 to 3, Articles 32, 38, 39, 40, 42, 43 and 44 and the Schedules, in force 21 November 2014; Parts 4, 5, 7, 8 (other than Article 32), Article 37 in force 1 May 2018 (R&O.17/2018);
-- Remainder in force 1 January 2019 (R&O.17/2018)."  R&O.17/2018 Article 1: the provisions "come into force on 1st May 2018"; Article 2: the remainder "come into force on 1st January 2019".
-- Stage 1 is brought into force by Article 44(2) of the Law as enacted, which is not among the sources, so the day it was made is not shown; the stand-in passed for it is the named day.
-- R&O.17/2018 was made on 6th March 2018 (its own heading).  Article 1(5) reads neither.
-- The enactment of each stage was made before 1 January 2019, so the text of the 1954 Law in force when it was made is the inferred one.
`the commencement of stage 1` MEANS `a commencement naming the day` (YMD 2014 11 21) (YMD 2014 11 21)
`the commencement of stage 2` MEANS `a commencement naming the day` (YMD 2018 5 1) (YMD 2018 3 6)
`the commencement of stage 3` MEANS `a commencement naming the day` (YMD 2019 1 1) (YMD 2018 3 6)

-- Positive control: Article 1(5) does not read the day the enactment was made.  The same stage 1 commencement with a different day of making gives the same day.
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` (`a commencement naming the day` (YMD 2014 11 21) (YMD 1999 1 1)) `the inferred text` EQUALS YMD 2014 11 21

-- Stage 1: 21 November 2014.
-- 1954 Article 1(5): the enactment comes into operation immediately on the expiration of the previous day, so its first day in force is the day it names.
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of stage 1` `the inferred text` EQUALS YMD 2014 11 21
-- The Charities row's own first day in force of stage 1 on the reading taken (the Table names 21 November 2014): the same day.
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of stage 1` `the inferred text` EQUALS `the first day in force of the stage` `stage 1: 21 November 2014` `fav`
-- The other reading (the day after the named day) gives 22 November 2014, which Article 1(5) does not give: the Law gives that reading no licence.
#ASSERT `the first day in force of the stage` `stage 1: 21 November 2014` `other F7.11` EQUALS YMD 2014 11 22
#ASSERT NOT (`the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of stage 1` `the inferred text` EQUALS `the first day in force of the stage` `stage 1: 21 November 2014` `other F7.11`)
-- 2021 Article 3(6): stage 1 commenced on 21 November 2014, before Part 2 did, so Article 3 does not affect it and the 1954 Law is the Law that governs.
#ASSERT NOT `Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` (JUST (YMD 2014 11 21)) `the first reading of L3`
-- Cross-check (hypothetical: the 2021 Law does not govern this day): Article 3(2) would have put the enactment into force at the beginning of the named day as well.
#ASSERT `Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` (YMD 2014 11 21) NOTHING `the first reading of L2` EQUALS `from the beginning of the day` (YMD 2014 11 21)

-- Stage 2: 1 May 2018 (R&O.17/2018 Article 1).
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of stage 2` `the inferred text` EQUALS YMD 2018 5 1
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of stage 2` `the inferred text` EQUALS `the first day in force of the stage` `stage 2: 1 May 2018` `fav`
#ASSERT `the first day in force of the stage` `stage 2: 1 May 2018` `other F7.11` EQUALS YMD 2018 5 2
#ASSERT NOT (`the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of stage 2` `the inferred text` EQUALS `the first day in force of the stage` `stage 2: 1 May 2018` `other F7.11`)
#ASSERT NOT `Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` (JUST (YMD 2018 5 1)) `the first reading of L3`
#ASSERT `Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` (YMD 2018 5 1) NOTHING `the first reading of L2` EQUALS `from the beginning of the day` (YMD 2018 5 1)

-- Stage 3: 1 January 2019 (R&O.17/2018 Article 2).
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of stage 3` `the inferred text` EQUALS YMD 2019 1 1
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of stage 3` `the inferred text` EQUALS `the first day in force of the stage` `stage 3: 1 January 2019` `fav`
#ASSERT `the first day in force of the stage` `stage 3: 1 January 2019` `other F7.11` EQUALS YMD 2019 1 2
#ASSERT NOT (`the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of stage 3` `the inferred text` EQUALS `the first day in force of the stage` `stage 3: 1 January 2019` `other F7.11`)
#ASSERT NOT `Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` (JUST (YMD 2019 1 1)) `the first reading of L3`
#ASSERT `Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` (YMD 2019 1 1) NOTHING `the first reading of L2` EQUALS `from the beginning of the day` (YMD 2019 1 1)

