§§ `Group 2a: Table rows that name a day, commenced before 28 September 2021 (F7.11)`

-- These four rows state the day in the Charities row's data as `on a day stated`, which is the form that fork F7.11 reads.  All four commenced on or after 1 January 2019 and before 28 September 2021:
-- 2021 Article 3(6) puts them outside Article 3, and the 1954 Law, in the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021, governs; the basis of that reading is the deposited text.
-- The dates are those of the Table of Legislation History in the endnotes of the Charities Law.

-- R&O.40/2019: the Table gives 31 May 2019; the Order's own heading says "Made 31st May 2019" and "Coming into force 31st May 2019".
`the commencement of R&O.40/2019` MEANS `a commencement naming the day` (YMD 2019 5 31) (YMD 2019 5 31)
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of R&O.40/2019` `the deposited text` EQUALS YMD 2019 5 31
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of R&O.40/2019` `the deposited text` EQUALS `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.40/2019` `fav`
#ASSERT `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.40/2019` `other F7.11` EQUALS YMD 2019 6 1
#ASSERT NOT (`the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of R&O.40/2019` `the deposited text` EQUALS `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.40/2019` `other F7.11`)
#ASSERT NOT `Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` (JUST (YMD 2019 5 31)) `the first reading of L3`
#ASSERT `Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` (YMD 2019 5 31) NOTHING `the first reading of L2` EQUALS `from the beginning of the day` (YMD 2019 5 31)

-- L.1/2017 (Dormant Bank Accounts (Jersey) Law 2017): the Table gives 16 December 2019.  Article 31(2) of that Law leaves the day to an Act of the States, which is not among the sources, so the day rests on the Table alone
-- and the day made is not shown: the stand-in passed for it is the named day.  Nor is it shown which text of the 1954 Law was in force when that Act was made; the basis passed is the deposited text, which only labels the answer.
`the commencement of L.1/2017` MEANS `a commencement naming the day` (YMD 2019 12 16) (YMD 2019 12 16)
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of L.1/2017` `the deposited text` EQUALS YMD 2019 12 16
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of L.1/2017` `the deposited text` EQUALS `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `L.1/2017` `fav`
#ASSERT `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `L.1/2017` `other F7.11` EQUALS YMD 2019 12 17
#ASSERT NOT (`the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of L.1/2017` `the deposited text` EQUALS `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `L.1/2017` `other F7.11`)
#ASSERT NOT `Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` (JUST (YMD 2019 12 16)) `the first reading of L3`
#ASSERT `Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` (YMD 2019 12 16) NOTHING `the first reading of L2` EQUALS `from the beginning of the day` (YMD 2019 12 16)

-- L.13/2019 (Revenue Administration (Jersey) Law 2019): the Table gives "1 January 2020 (R&O.136/2019)".  R&O.136/2019 Article 1 brings the Law into force on 1st January 2020 (other than Articles 17, 18 and 19);
-- that Act was made on 10th December 2019 (its own heading), and it is the enactment that names the day.
`the commencement of L.13/2019` MEANS `a commencement naming the day` (YMD 2020 1 1) (YMD 2019 12 10)
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of L.13/2019` `the deposited text` EQUALS YMD 2020 1 1
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of L.13/2019` `the deposited text` EQUALS `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `L.13/2019` `fav`
#ASSERT `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `L.13/2019` `other F7.11` EQUALS YMD 2020 1 2
#ASSERT NOT (`the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of L.13/2019` `the deposited text` EQUALS `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `L.13/2019` `other F7.11`)
#ASSERT NOT `Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` (JUST (YMD 2020 1 1)) `the first reading of L3`
#ASSERT `Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` (YMD 2020 1 1) NOTHING `the first reading of L2` EQUALS `from the beginning of the day` (YMD 2020 1 1)

-- R&O.29/2021: the Table gives 2 March 2021; the Order's own heading says "Made 2nd March 2021" and "Coming into force 2nd March 2021".
`the commencement of R&O.29/2021` MEANS `a commencement naming the day` (YMD 2021 3 2) (YMD 2021 3 2)
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of R&O.29/2021` `the deposited text` EQUALS YMD 2021 3 2
#ASSERT `the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of R&O.29/2021` `the deposited text` EQUALS `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.29/2021` `fav`
#ASSERT `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.29/2021` `other F7.11` EQUALS YMD 2021 3 3
#ASSERT NOT (`the day that Article 1(5) of the 1954 Law gives` `the commencement of R&O.29/2021` `the deposited text` EQUALS `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.29/2021` `other F7.11`)
#ASSERT NOT `Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` (JUST (YMD 2021 3 2)) `the first reading of L3`
#ASSERT `Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` (YMD 2021 3 2) NOTHING `the first reading of L2` EQUALS `from the beginning of the day` (YMD 2021 3 2)

§§ `Group 2b: Table rows that state a period, commenced on or after 28 September 2021 (not F7.11)`

-- No row of the Table both names a day in the enactment's own words and commenced on or after 28 September 2021.  The three rows that did commence then are the three Orders that come into force "7 days after it is made",
-- which name no day: the Table gives the day that results (R&O.28/2023 made 5 April 2023, R&O.56/2025 made 9 October 2025, R&O.102/2026 made 25 September 2026).
-- Fork F7.11 is not read for these rows by the Charities row (it reads F7.2 instead), and the Laws copied here do not say how a period is counted (the 1954 subject records the same), so the groups below do not settle F7.2.
-- What they assert holds whichever way the seven days are counted: 2021 Article 3(6) puts a commencement on or after 28 September 2021 inside Article 3; and IF the day the Table gives is taken as the particular day the Order is expressed to come into force on
-- (assumed, not ruled), Article 3(2) gives the beginning of that day; and the Charities row gives that day on both readings of F7.11.

-- R&O.28/2023: the Table gives 12 April 2023.
#ASSERT `Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` (JUST (YMD 2023 4 12)) `the first reading of L3`
#ASSERT `Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` (YMD 2023 4 12) NOTHING `the first reading of L2` EQUALS `from the beginning of the day` (YMD 2023 4 12)
#ASSERT `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.28/2023` `fav` EQUALS YMD 2023 4 12
#ASSERT `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.28/2023` `other F7.11` EQUALS YMD 2023 4 12

-- R&O.56/2025: the Table gives 16 October 2025.
#ASSERT `Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` (JUST (YMD 2025 10 16)) `the first reading of L3`
#ASSERT `Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` (YMD 2025 10 16) NOTHING `the first reading of L2` EQUALS `from the beginning of the day` (YMD 2025 10 16)
#ASSERT `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.56/2025` `fav` EQUALS YMD 2025 10 16
#ASSERT `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.56/2025` `other F7.11` EQUALS YMD 2025 10 16

-- R&O.102/2026: the Table gives 2 October 2026.
#ASSERT `Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` (JUST (YMD 2026 10 2)) `the first reading of L3`
#ASSERT `Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` (YMD 2026 10 2) NOTHING `the first reading of L2` EQUALS `from the beginning of the day` (YMD 2026 10 2)
#ASSERT `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.102/2026` `fav` EQUALS YMD 2026 10 2
#ASSERT `the first day in force that the Charities row gives to` `R&O.102/2026` `other F7.11` EQUALS YMD 2026 10 2

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- (3) F7.14: an Order under Article 2(2) and a consequential amendment of the Charities Law
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§ `Group 3: an Order and the Charities Law (F7.14)`

-- Charities Law Article 2(2): "The Minister may by Order add sub-paragraphs to paragraph (1)."  Article 39(1) lets an Order contain transitional, consequential, incidental, supplementary or savings provision "other than an amendment of this Law";
-- Article 39(2) extends a power to amend this Law "by Regulations" to the consequential amendments of other provisions of it.  Article 39(1) therefore says in terms what the reading taken says.
-- The 2021 Law, Article 9(5): "Unless provision is made to the contrary, a power to make Jersey subordinate legislation also authorises the making of provision incidental to, supplemental to, or consequential upon that legislation."
-- Article 9(6): "However, the power in paragraph (5) is limited (a) in the case of Regulations, to amending any legislation other than the Human Rights (Jersey) Law 2000 or the legislation giving the power to make the Regulations;
-- and (b) in the case of any legislation not made by the States, to amending any other legislation not made by the States."
-- An Order of the Minister is signed, not adopted by the States Assembly (2021 Article 2(3)(b)), so it is legislation not made by the States; the Charities Law is a Law, made by the States.
-- The 2021 Law applies to a power to make Jersey subordinate legislation conferred "whenever made" (Article 9(1)) and came into force on 28 September 2021; R&O.56/2025, made on 9 October 2025 by the Minister for External Relations
-- under Article 2(2) of the Charities Law, is the one Order made under that Article in the sources, and it inserted paragraph (ba) in Article 2(1) and changed nothing else.
-- No provision to the contrary of Article 9(5) and (6) for an Order is found in the Charities Law as this row encodes it: Article 39(1) says the opposite.

-- The two readings of "made by the States" (fork L5 of the 2021 subject) and the two readings of whether the limits bear on provision that is not an amendment (fork L6).
`L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts` MEANS `made by the States means adopted by the States Assembly, as Article 2(1)(a) and 2(3)(a) put it, so it covers Laws, Regulations and Acts`
`L5, made by the States means a Law only` MEANS `made by the States means a Law only`
`L6, the limits bear on amendments only` MEANS `the limits bear on provision that amends other legislation, and incidental provision of any other kind is not limited by them`
`L6, the power is limited to amendments` MEANS `the power in Article 9(5) is limited to amendments of the permitted kind, and authorises incidental provision of no other kind`

-- The Charities Law as the legislation to be amended: a Law, not the Human Rights (Jersey) Law 2000; in the first, it is also the legislation giving the power to make the instrument (an Order under its own Article 2(2), or Regulations under it).
`the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power` MEANS
    `Legislation to be amended` WITH
        `its form` IS `a Law`
        `it is the Human Rights (Jersey) Law 2000` IS FALSE
        `it is the legislation giving the power to make the instrument` IS TRUE

`the Charities Law, when another Law gives the power` MEANS
    `Legislation to be amended` WITH
        `its form` IS `a Law`
        `it is the Human Rights (Jersey) Law 2000` IS FALSE
        `it is the legislation giving the power to make the instrument` IS FALSE

-- What the 2021 Law does for a provision that amends the Charities Law, made by an instrument of a given form.
GIVEN form IS A `Form of legislation`
      amended IS A `Legislation to be amended`
      contrary IS A BOOLEAN
      l5 IS A `Reading L5 of legislation made by the States`
      l6 IS A `Reading L6 of the limits in Article 9(6)`
GIVETH A `What the incidental power does`
`what the 2021 Law does for an amendment of the Charities Law by` form amended contrary l5 l6 MEANS
    `Article 9(5) and (6): what the incidental power does for the provision` form amended TRUE contrary l5 l6

-- Whether the 2021 Law authorises an Order, made under the Charities Law, to amend the Charities Law, where no provision is made to the contrary.
GIVEN l5 IS A `Reading L5 of legislation made by the States`
      l6 IS A `Reading L6 of the limits in Article 9(6)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the 2021 Law authorises an Order to amend the Charities Law` l5 l6 MEANS
    `what the 2021 Law does for an amendment of the Charities Law by` `an Order` `the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power` FALSE l5 l6 EQUALS `Article 9(5) authorises the provision`

-- The Order barred, on both readings of L5 and both readings of L6 (L6 is read only for provision that is not an amendment, so it does not matter here).
-- 2021 Article 9(6)(b): the Order is not made by the States, so its power is limited to amending other legislation not made by the States; the Charities Law is made by the States, on either reading of L5.
#ASSERT `what the 2021 Law does for an amendment of the Charities Law by` `an Order` `the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power` FALSE `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only` EQUALS `Article 9(6) bars the provision`
#ASSERT `what the 2021 Law does for an amendment of the Charities Law by` `an Order` `the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power` FALSE `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts` `L6, the power is limited to amendments` EQUALS `Article 9(6) bars the provision`
#ASSERT `what the 2021 Law does for an amendment of the Charities Law by` `an Order` `the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power` FALSE `L5, made by the States means a Law only` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only` EQUALS `Article 9(6) bars the provision`
#ASSERT `what the 2021 Law does for an amendment of the Charities Law by` `an Order` `the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power` FALSE `L5, made by the States means a Law only` `L6, the power is limited to amendments` EQUALS `Article 9(6) bars the provision`
-- The bar is Article 9(6)(b) and no other limit: 9(6)(a) speaks of Regulations only, so an Order meets it.
#ASSERT NOT `the limit in Article 9(6)(b) is met` `an Order` `the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power` `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts`
#ASSERT NOT `the limit in Article 9(6)(b) is met` `an Order` `the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power` `L5, made by the States means a Law only`
#ASSERT `the limit in Article 9(6)(a) is met` `an Order` `the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power`
-- 2021 Article 2(1)(a) and 2(3)(a), (b): a Law is made by the States on either reading of L5; an Order is signed and is not.
#ASSERT `Article 2: the legislation was made by the States` `a Law` `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts`
#ASSERT `Article 2: the legislation was made by the States` `a Law` `L5, made by the States means a Law only`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 2: the legislation was made by the States` `an Order` `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 2: the legislation was made by the States` `an Order` `L5, made by the States means a Law only`

-- The Charities row's answer to the question, on each reading of F7.14, written by hand: an Order under Article 2(2) may NOT make a consequential amendment of another provision of the Law on the reading taken, and may on the other.
#ASSERT NOT `Art 39 an Order under Article 2(2) may make a consequential amendment of another provision of the Law` `fav`
#ASSERT `Art 39 an Order under Article 2(2) may make a consequential amendment of another provision of the Law` `other F7.14`
-- The 2021 Law's answer (an Order is not authorised) is the Charities row's answer on the reading taken, on either reading of L5.
#ASSERT NOT `the 2021 Law authorises an Order to amend the Charities Law` `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only`
#ASSERT NOT `the 2021 Law authorises an Order to amend the Charities Law` `L5, made by the States means a Law only` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only`
#ASSERT `the 2021 Law authorises an Order to amend the Charities Law` `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only` EQUALS `Art 39 an Order under Article 2(2) may make a consequential amendment of another provision of the Law` `fav`
#ASSERT `the 2021 Law authorises an Order to amend the Charities Law` `L5, made by the States means a Law only` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only` EQUALS `Art 39 an Order under Article 2(2) may make a consequential amendment of another provision of the Law` `fav`
-- The other reading of F7.14 (an Order can too) differs from the 2021 Law's answer on both readings of L5: the 2021 Law gives it no support.
#ASSERT NOT (`the 2021 Law authorises an Order to amend the Charities Law` `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only` EQUALS `Art 39 an Order under Article 2(2) may make a consequential amendment of another provision of the Law` `other F7.14`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the 2021 Law authorises an Order to amend the Charities Law` `L5, made by the States means a Law only` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only` EQUALS `Art 39 an Order under Article 2(2) may make a consequential amendment of another provision of the Law` `other F7.14`)
-- The only route to the other reading is a provision to the contrary in the Charities Law, which makes Article 9(5) not apply and leaves the 2021 Law with no answer (9(5): "Unless provision is made to the contrary").
-- Article 39(1) of the Charities Law, encoded in g7-art37-43-final, bars an amendment of the Law from the ancillary provisions of an Order, so no such provision is found.
#ASSERT `what the 2021 Law does for an amendment of the Charities Law by` `an Order` `the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power` TRUE `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only` EQUALS `Article 9(5) does not apply because provision is made to the contrary`
#ASSERT NOT `Art 39(1) the provision may be contained in the Order or Regulations` `an amendment of this Law` TRUE

-- Regulations amending the Charities Law are a different case: that they are not barred by 9(6)(b) holds on the first reading of L5 only.
-- On the first reading of L5 the Regulations are made by the States, so 9(6)(b) does not bar them; 9(6)(a) bars them only where the Charities Law is the legislation giving the power to make the Regulations.
-- On the second reading of L5 ("a Law only") Regulations are legislation not made by the States, and 9(6)(b) bars Regulations from amending a Law too.
-- Whether Article 39(2) of the Charities Law is itself a provision to the contrary that displaces 9(6)(a) for Regulations made under the Charities Law is not tested here.
#ASSERT `the limit in Article 9(6)(b) is met` `Regulations` `the Charities Law, when another Law gives the power` `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts`
#ASSERT NOT `the limit in Article 9(6)(b) is met` `Regulations` `the Charities Law, when another Law gives the power` `L5, made by the States means a Law only`
#ASSERT `the limit in Article 9(6)(a) is met` `Regulations` `the Charities Law, when another Law gives the power`
#ASSERT NOT `the limit in Article 9(6)(a) is met` `Regulations` `the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power`
#ASSERT `what the 2021 Law does for an amendment of the Charities Law by` `Regulations` `the Charities Law, when another Law gives the power` FALSE `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only` EQUALS `Article 9(5) authorises the provision`
#ASSERT `what the 2021 Law does for an amendment of the Charities Law by` `Regulations` `the Charities Law, as the legislation giving the power` FALSE `L5, made by the States covers Laws, Regulations and Acts` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only` EQUALS `Article 9(6) bars the provision`
#ASSERT `what the 2021 Law does for an amendment of the Charities Law by` `Regulations` `the Charities Law, when another Law gives the power` FALSE `L5, made by the States means a Law only` `L6, the limits bear on amendments only` EQUALS `Article 9(6) bars the provision`
